I believe AI will likely evolve into six distinct tiers:

1) Top Secret / National Security Tier
Serving:
*  Intelligence;
*  Military;
*  Nuclear, biological, and cyber domains;
*  Space operations;
*  Strategic wargaming;
*  Counter-intelligence;
*  Large-scale surveillance and defense.
Models in this category will not be made public; even standard government agencies may not have access to them.

2) Government / Defense Contractor Tier
Used for:
*  Defense procurement;
*  Battlefield logistics;
*  Weapon system testing;
*  Vulnerability research;
*  Intelligence summarization;
*  Crisis simulation;
*  Critical infrastructure protection.
Access at this tier will be tied to identity verification, audit trails, activity logs, specific permissions, geographic location, and nationality.

3) Private Enterprise Tier (Critical Sectors)
Major corporations will integrate AI with their proprietary data:
*  Financial institutions;
*  Pharmaceutical companies;
*  Energy companies;
*  Chip manufacturers;
*  Cloud providers;
*  Law firms;
*  Healthcare groups;
*  Industrial software companies.
The value of this tier may far exceed that of the public version, as it incorporates private data, workflows, toolchains, and closed-loop feedback mechanisms.

4) Controlled Research Tier
Provided to vetted entities such as universities, laboratories, cybersecurity teams, and biosafety teams.
This aligns with initiatives like Anthropic’s Project Glasswing and Mythos. Anthropic has stated that its Glasswing partners used Mythos Preview to discover over 10,000 high-risk or critical vulnerabilities in globally critical software.
Capabilities of this magnitude cannot simply be released to the public in the same way as standard chatbots. 5) Premium Commercial/Enterprise Versions
Examples include Plus, Pro, Team, and Enterprise tiers.
These are highly capable but come with strict limitations in sensitive areas.

